<<setup,echo=FALSE,results=hide>>=
library(gpim)
options(width = 72, digits = 3)
@

\section{Observed event histories}

The package fits probabilistic index regression for prioritized survival
outcomes. Death is compared first. A nonfatal component is considered only
when all higher-priority components are tied. A later event time is favorable,
and a genuine tie receives the midpoint response \(1/2\).

Each individual contributes exactly one terminal row. Status 1 identifies
death and status 0 identifies censoring. Each observed nonfatal event has a
separate row, with code 2 for the highest-priority nonfatal component.
Additional positive codes identify further nonfatal components for CHW and
RCW. Baseline covariates are constant within an individual's records.
Observation of nonfatal events ends at death or censoring. If a nonfatal
event is not observed, its event row is omitted.

The supplied \texttt{hfaction} and \texttt{hierarchy} data sets are synthetic.
Neither contains original clinical participant records. The former contains
death and first hospitalization, with time in years. The latter illustrates
three endpoint priorities.

<<data>>=
data(hfaction, package = "gpim")
head(hfaction[, c("id", "time", "status", "treatment")])
@

The default priority order follows increasing positive status codes.
An explicit \texttt{priorities} vector must begin with 1 and include every
observed positive code. Each nonfatal component is time to its first event.
The option \texttt{recurrent = "first"} uses the first occurrence when a type
has repeated records. Use \texttt{recurrent = "error"} to reject repeated
records. Missing required values are rejected by default.
The option \texttt{na.action = "na.omit"} removes the whole individual when
any required value is missing.

\section{Three estimating methods}

Complete-history weighting, requested as \texttt{"CHW"}, uses pairs for which
both restricted histories are observed. Resolved-comparison weighting,
requested as \texttt{"RCW"}, also uses comparisons determined before both
histories are complete. These methods require a fitted censoring model.

Conditional tie weighting, requested as \texttt{"CTW"}, additionally uses
an observed nonfatal ordering when the fatal tie remains uncertain. Its
conditional probability weights the complete regression residual for that
ordering. A joint event model supplies this probability.
Fang and Li~\cite{FangLi2026} develop conditional tie weighting, and
Fang et al.~\cite{Fang2026} develop the regression equations.

The built-in CTW models cover one fatal event and one first nonfatal event.
CHW and RCW also allow the larger fixed hierarchy represented by
\texttt{hierarchy}. Under their respective model assumptions, the untruncated
equations target the same full data regression parameter. The package does
not assert a general ordering of the fitted estimators' variances.

\section{Fitting a model}

The PIM formula uses differences between individual model-matrix rows.
A free intercept therefore cancels. Factors, transformations and interactions
follow the usual R formula conventions. This example compares all three
methods at \(L=2\) years.

<<fit>>=
event_model <- pim_joint_model(
  copula = "gumbel",
  margins = c(death = "weibull", nonfatal = "weibull"),
  death_formula = ~ treatment,
  nonfatal_formula = ~ treatment
)

fit <- gpim(
  HierSurv(time, status) ~ treatment + I(age / 10),
  data = hfaction,
  id = "id",
  L = 2,
  method = c("CHW", "RCW", "CTW"),
  link = "logit",
  censor_formula = ~ treatment + I(age / 10),
  joint_model = event_model
)
@

The age coefficient corresponds to a ten-year increase. Under the logit link,
exponentiating a coefficient gives the conditional win odds for its covariate
contrast. The treatment contrast follows the factor coding in the model
matrix.

<<summary>>=
tab <- summary(fit, method = "CTW")$coefficients
print(tab[, c("term", "estimate", "std.error", "conf.low", "conf.high")],
      digits = 3, row.names = FALSE)
@

The package also supports \texttt{"probit"} and \texttt{"identity"}.
For a covariate difference \(z\), their inverse links are
\(\Phi(z^\top\beta)\) and \((1+z^\top\beta)/2\), respectively.
Identity coefficients are on the conditional net-benefit scale. A vector of
links or restriction times requests several fits in one call.

<<several-fits,eval=FALSE>>=
fit_several <- gpim(
  HierSurv(time, status) ~ treatment + I(age / 10),
  data = hfaction,
  id = "id",
  L = c(1.5, 2),
  method = "all",
  link = c("logit", "probit"),
  censor_formula = ~ treatment + I(age / 10),
  joint_model = event_model
)
@

Use prespecified restriction times when inference at those times is the
scientific objective. If \texttt{L} is omitted, the package uses the 25th,
50th and 75th sample quantiles of the individual terminal times. Inference
treats the selected times as fixed, and confidence intervals are pointwise.

\section{Joint event models for CTW}

The model specification is supplied through \texttt{pim\_joint\_model()}.
Copula choices are \texttt{"gumbel"}, \texttt{"clayton"},
\texttt{"frank"}, \texttt{"plackett"} and \texttt{"independence"}.
Gumbel, Clayton and Frank options use positive dependence. Plackett allows
positive or negative dependence. Independence fixes the dependence structure
while estimating the margin parameters.

Margin choices are \texttt{"weibull"}, \texttt{"lognormal"},
\texttt{"exponential"} and \texttt{"cox"}. The first entry of
\texttt{margins} specifies death and the second specifies the nonfatal
component. A single entry is repeated for both components. Named entries
\texttt{death} and \texttt{nonfatal} make the order explicit. The two
components can have different margin types and different baseline covariates.
Weibull, exponential and Cox margins use proportional hazards. Lognormal
margins use accelerated failure time regression. Parametric margins require
positive event times.

<<cox-margins,eval=FALSE>>=
cox_event_model <- pim_joint_model(
  copula = "frank",
  margins = c(death = "cox", nonfatal = "cox"),
  death_formula = ~ treatment + I(age / 10),
  nonfatal_formula = ~ treatment + I(age / 10)
)

fit_cox <- gpim(
  HierSurv(time, status) ~ treatment + I(age / 10),
  data = hfaction,
  id = "id",
  L = 2,
  method = "CTW",
  censor_formula = ~ treatment + I(age / 10),
  joint_model = cox_event_model
)
@

Cox margin functions leave the baseline cumulative hazards unspecified.
Their increments are estimated jointly with the regression coefficients
and copula parameter. The likelihood includes death without a preceding
nonfatal event and assigns no nonfatal event after death. The second copula
margin function is part of this joint construction. It is not obtained by
treating death as independent censoring in a separate nonfatal-event Cox fit.

The event model is fitted once across requested restriction times and links.
The conditional tie probability at \(L\) uses the corresponding restricted
individual history. Follow-up beyond \(L\) can estimate the common event-model
parameters. When an event formula is omitted, its margin uses the PIM
formula's predictors. The function \texttt{pim\_joint\_fit(fit)} returns
the fitted event model.

Each selected copula is a separate model specification. The package does not
choose a copula automatically. Scientific interpretation and sensitivity to
alternative families remain part of the analysis.

\section{Censoring adjustment and variance}

The censoring model uses Cox regression and Breslow ties. Its covariates are
given by \texttt{censor\_formula}. A formula \texttt{\~{}1} specifies a
model without covariates. Weights use the left limit of the fitted cumulative
censoring hazard at the required comparison time.

All three methods use the subject influence covariance. For method \(a\),
let \(\widehat v_{L,i}^a\) be the fitted coefficient influence of individual
\(i\), and let \(\overline v_L^a\) be its average. Then
\begin{equation*}
\widehat{\operatorname{Var}}(\widehat\beta_L^a)
=\frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^n
(\widehat v_{L,i}^a-\overline v_L^a)
(\widehat v_{L,i}^a-\overline v_L^a)^\top.
\end{equation*}
The influence includes the pair projection and the censoring contribution.
CTW adds the joint event-model contribution. These are added before forming
the covariance, including their cross covariances.

For censoring, the coefficient and baseline contributions use the fitted
martingale increments \(d\widehat M_i^C(t)=dN_i^C(t)-
Y_i^C(t)\exp(\widehat\gamma^\top W_i)d\widehat\Lambda_0^C(t)\).
The Cox coefficient and the Breslow baseline both contribute to uncertainty
in the weights. The joint-model contribution uses individual likelihood
scores and the negative Hessian of the mean log likelihood. With Cox
margins, the score includes both baseline cumulative hazards as well as the
regression and dependence parameters. The baseline functions and conditional
tie probabilities are not treated as fixed.

Version 0.1.0 uses this formula throughout. Its finite-sample values can differ
from the previous overlapping-pair covariance calculation. The two formulas
have the same first-order asymptotic representation under the stated
assumptions.

The centered coefficient influence matrix is stored with each fitted
component and reproduces its covariance directly.

<<influence,eval=FALSE>>=
component <- fit$fits[[1]]
crossprod(component$influence) / fit$n^2
component$vcov
names(component$influence_components)
@

\section{Extraction and controls}

Standard extractors allow selection by method, link and restriction time.

<<extract,eval=FALSE>>=
coef(fit, method = "CTW")
vcov(fit, method = "CTW")
confint(fit, method = "CTW")
trajectory_data(fit)
pim_joint_fit(fit)
saveRDS(fit, "fit.rds")
@

PIM options are supplied through \texttt{pim\_control()} and event-model
options through \texttt{pim\_joint\_model()}. Their help pages list the
defaults. A positive probability floor changes the censoring weights when
active. Use \texttt{weight\_floor = 0} for untruncated weights.

<<controls,eval=FALSE>>=
control <- pim_control(weight_floor = 0, maxit = 200, trace = TRUE)
event_model <- pim_joint_model(
  copula = "clayton",
  margins = "weibull",
  maxit = 1000,
  trace = TRUE
)
@

Pair and likelihood calculations use C++17. For Cox event-model margins,
the number of baseline increments grows with the number of distinct event
times. Standard errors include these additional estimated parameters.

